# XSH CMPLX(), CMPLXF() and CMPLXL()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Components are compared with creal()/cimag() and their float and long
# double forms; signs of zero with signbit(); NaN with isnan(); infinity
# with isinf().

assertion  XSH.CMPLX.001
anchor     functions/CMPLX.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/CMPLX.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       CMPLX(x, y), CMPLXF(x, y) and CMPLXL(x, y) have the real part x
           and imaginary part y (converted to the type), for several finite
           values including integers passed as arguments.
test       t/001-components.c

assertion  XSH.CMPLX.002
anchor     functions/CMPLX.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       The parts are exact for the special values that x + I*y would
           damage: CMPLX(-0.0, -0.0) and CMPLX(0.0, -0.0) keep the signs
           of both zeros; CMPLX(INFINITY, 1.0) has an infinite real part
           and imaginary part 1.0 (not NaN); CMPLX(1.0, INFINITY) and
           CMPLX(NAN, 2.0), CMPLX(3.0, NAN) keep the finite part exactly;
           the same for the float and long double forms.
test       t/002-special-values.c

assertion  XSH.CMPLX.003
anchor     functions/CMPLX.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       The expressions have the types double complex, float complex and
           long double complex respectively, whatever the argument types.
test       t/003-types.c

assertion  XSH.CMPLX.004
anchor     functions/CMPLX.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       The expressions can initialise objects with static storage
           duration (file scope and function-local static) from constant
           arguments, and arguments are converted to the type of the macro:
           CMPLXF(0.1, 0.2) has the float values of 0.1 and 0.2.
test       t/004-static-and-conversion.c
